   "h": "Questions validation and engineering teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "Who builds the still, and how do we check its quality?",
     "a": "[still builder: in-house or named maker]. Ask for the sample turnover package: weld map, weld log, borescope records, material certificates by heat number and pressure vessel documents. Your QA team can audit [the shop that builds the still] and witness its hold points, and your quality unit approves the FAT protocol before we run it."
    },
    {
     "q": "When do the FAT and IQ/OQ documents reach us?",
     "a": "The FAT protocol reaches you [protocol lead time, weeks] before the test. It covers sequences, alarms, interlocks, start-up divert, instrument loops and [hot run scope at FAT]. The executed report and deviation log arrive before the still ships, and the IQ/OQ protocols with it, so your team is not writing them during start-up."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why distil when Ph. Eur. now allows membrane WFI?",
     "a": "Since April 2017, Ph. Eur. 0169 accepts reverse osmosis coupled with other suitable techniques; USP has long accepted any process equivalent or superior to distillation. Distil for a hot loop, rely on the longest inspection record, or have steam or cheap power. Membrane saves energy where ambient storage suits. We model both when the choice is close."
    },
    {
     "q": "How much endotoxin reduction can we count on?",
     "a": "Only what the still manufacturer guarantees, which FDA's inspection guide puts at 2.5 to 3 log for many stills. A 3 log still fed at 250 EU/mL delivers 0.25 EU/mL, which already fails the < 0.25 EU/mL limit. So we set a PW endotoxin target well below that and design the PW system and its sanitization to hold it."
    },
    {
     "q": "Does an imported still need a Canadian registration number?",
     "a": "Yes. Pressure vessels and fittings installed in Canada need a CRN under CSA B51 from the province of installation, and a still designed for another market may need design changes first. Registration is in the project schedule from the start, in [provinces where Aquemi registers]."
    },
    {
     "q": "What happens at start-up and shutdown?",
     "a": "Every start-up diverts distillate to drain until conductivity and temperature reach setpoint, and the condenser and distillate line drain on shutdown. FDA's inspection guide describes a still whose condenser held about 50 litres of WFI for up to several days between runs, a source of endotoxin; we design so that cannot happen."
    },
    {
     "q": "Do hot WFI systems rouge, and who deals with it?",
     "a": "Hot WFI and pure steam systems develop rouge, an iron oxide film on stainless steel, over time. We record the surface condition at handover and include inspection in the service plan, with derouging and repassivation when trends call for it."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who services the still after handover?",
